<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RUT_ForgePulse_BiomeWiring.xml         UtinniPatches - FORGE_MECHANICS_1 F1

  Wires the permanent weather pulse (Defs/GameConditionDefs/
  RUT_ForgePulse.xml) onto RUT_TheForge as a PATCH rather than a direct
  edit to RUT_TheForge.xml's own biomeMapConditions field -
  RUT_ForgePulse only exists when mandrake.rm.environmentalhazards is
  active (MayRequire-gated def), so a bare <li> baked directly into the
  BiomeDef would be a dangling cross-reference the moment that mod is
  absent. MayRequire on the Operation itself makes this patch a true
  no-op (not merely a silently-false match) when the mod is missing.
  Same pattern SCALD_MECHANICS_1's RUT_ScaldSteamLock_BiomeWiring.xml
  already established for this exact file family.

  RUT_TheForge.xml carries no <biomeMapConditions> node at all today (only
  wildAnimals/baseWeatherCommonalities/terrainsByFertility/diseases), so
  this adds the whole element rather than appending an <li> to an existing
  one. RUT_TheForge.xml's own baseWeatherCommonalities (AB_VolcanicAsh/
  AB_VolcanicAshRain/RSW_SW_RedFog/Clear) is left untouched by this patch:
  once this condition registers, GameCondition.ForcedWeather() overrides
  natural weather selection unconditionally for as long as the condition
  runs (permanent, per BiomeConditionMapComponent), so those commonality
  entries go structurally unreachable rather than wrong - editing that
  table is biome-authoring judgment outside this build pass's scope, not
  touched here.
-->
<Patch>

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationAdd" MayRequire="mandrake.rm.environmentalhazards">
    <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="RUT_TheForge"]</xpath>
    <value>
      <biomeMapConditions>
        <li>RUT_ForgePulse</li>
      </biomeMapConditions>
    </value>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
